/// Translate an APNs HTTP status into the outcome the instance acts on.
///
/// This mapping is the whole back-channel: it is the only thing that tells a Custos
/// instance to delete a registration, to back off, or to try again later. See the
/// contribution note below — the body of this function is deliberately left to be written
/// with the pruning policy decided.
fn classify_status(status: u16) -> PushOutcome {
    // TODO(contribution): map an APNs status to a `PushOutcome`.
    //
    // The statuses Apple actually returns on the provider API:
    //   200  accepted
    //   400  malformed request — reason names the fault, e.g. `BadDeviceToken`,
    //        `BadTopic`, `DeviceTokenNotForTopic`, `PayloadTooLarge`
    //   403  the provider token is bad (wrong key, wrong team, expired, malformed)
    //   404  bad request path
    //   410  `Unregistered` — the token is dead and must not be used again
    //   413  payload too large
    //   429  `TooManyRequests` for this device token
    //   500/503  Apple-side failure, retryable
    //
    // The issue pins four of these: 200 → Delivered, 410 → Unregistered,
    // 429/5xx → ApnsError. The judgement is what to do with the rest — in particular
    // whether a 400 (which Apple uses for `BadDeviceToken`, a token that was never valid
    // for this topic) should also report `Unregistered` so the instance prunes it, or
    // stay `ApnsError` so a relay misconfiguration is never mistaken for a dead device.
    //
    // Note that `PushOutcome::Unregistered` is a *destructive* instruction: on receipt the
    // instance deletes the device's registration row, and the device gets no notifications
    // again until it re-registers on next launch.
    let _ = status;
    todo!("classify_status")
}

